"To solve the equation \\( |5x - 1| = |3x + 2|\\), we need to consider the properties of absolute values. The absolute value function is non-negative, so we can equate the expressions inside the absolute values:\n\n\\[ 5x - 1 = 3x + 2 \\]\n\nNext, we solve this equation for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ 5x - 1 = 3x + 2 \\]\n\nSubtract \\( 3x \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[ 2x - 1 = 2 \\]\n\nAdd 1 to both sides:\n\n\\[ 2x = 3 \\]\n\nDivide by 2:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nThus, the smallest value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}} \\]"
"First, solve the inequality $|x| + 1 > 7$:\n\\[\n|x| > 6 \\implies x < -6 \\text{ or } x > 6.\n\\]\n\nNext, solve the inequality $|x+1| \\le 7$:\n\\[\n|x+1| \\le 7 \\implies -7 \\le x+1 \\le 7 \\implies -8 \\le x \\le 6.\n\\]\n\nThe solution of these two inequalities is the intersection of the intervals:\n\\[\nx < -6 \\text{ or } x > 6 \\text{ and } -8 \\le x \\le 6 \\implies -8 \\le x \\le 6.\n\\]\n\nThe integers that satisfy this condition are $-8, -7, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5$. The sum of these integers is:\n\\[\n-8 + (-7) + (-2) + (-1) + 0 + 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 = -8.\n\\]\n\nThus, the sum is $\\boxed{-8}$."
