"We start with the given equation:\n\\[\n\\frac{A\\sqrt{B}}{C} = \\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we simplify the right-hand side:\n\\[\n\\frac{9}{2\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[\n\\frac{A\\sqrt{B}}{C} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\\[\nA\\sqrt{B} = 3C\n\\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(C\\) have no common prime factors, \\(B\\) has no perfect-square factors other than 1, and \\(A\\sqrt{B} = 3C\\), we can infer that \\(A = 3\\), \\(B = 3\\), and \\(C = 2\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(A + B + C\\) is:\n\\[\nA + B + C = 3 + 3 + 2 = 8\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]"
