"To find the value of \\( x \\) for which \\(\\frac{2x-1}{2x+2} = \\frac{x-3}{x-1}\\), we start by setting the two expressions equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2x-1}{2x+2} = \\frac{x-3}{x-1}\n\\]\n\nNext, we cross-multiply to eliminate the fractions:\n\n\\[\n(2x-1)(x-1) = (x-3)(2x+2)\n\\]\n\nExpanding both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n2x^2 - 3x + 1 = 2x^2 - 4x - 6\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\( 2x^2 \\) from both sides and both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n-3x + 1 = -4x - 6\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n1 = -7\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x \\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-7}\n\\]"
"The distance traveled by the horse 64 feet from the center is the circumference of the circle with radius 64 feet, which is $2\\pi(64) = 128\\pi$ feet. Since the horse makes 27 revolutions, the total distance traveled is $27(128\\pi) = 3456\\pi$ feet.\n\nThe distance traveled by the horse 16 feet from the center is the circumference of the circle with radius 16 feet, which is $2\\pi(16) = 32\\pi$ feet. If the horse makes $n$ revolutions, the total distance traveled is $n(32\\pi) = 3456\\pi$ feet. Solving for $n$, we get $n = \\frac{3456\\pi}{32\\pi} = 108$.\n\nTherefore, the horse 16 feet from the center would have to make $\\boxed{108}$ revolutions."
