"Let the weight of the two smaller dogs be \\( x \\) pounds. Then the weight of the larger dog is the smaller weight plus 20 pounds, which is \\( x + 20 \\) pounds.\n\nThe combined weight of the three dogs is given as 185 pounds. Therefore, we can write the equation:\n\n\\[ x + x + (x + 20) = 185 \\]\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\n\\[ 3x + 20 = 185 \\]\n\nSubtract 20 from both sides:\n\n\\[ 3x = 165 \\]\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[ x = 55 \\]\n\nSo, the weight of the two smaller dogs is 55 pounds, and the weight of the larger dog is:\n\n\\[ 55 + 20 = 75 \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of the largest dog is \\(\\boxed{75}\\) pounds."
"Let the roots of the quadratic equation $x^2 + bx + c = 0$ be $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$. By Vieta's formulas, we know:\n\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = -b$$\n$$\\alpha \\beta = c$$\n\nThe difference of the roots is given by $|\\alpha - \\beta| = |b - 2c|$. Using the identity for the difference of roots, we have:\n\n$$|\\alpha - \\beta| = \\sqrt{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 4\\alpha \\beta} = \\sqrt{b^2 - 4c}$$\n\nSubstituting the given condition, we get:\n\n$$\\sqrt{b^2 - 4c} = |b - 2c|$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we obtain:\n\n$$b^2 - 4c = (b - 2c)^2$$\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we get:\n\n$$b^2 - 4c = b^2 - 4bc + 4c^2$$\n\n$$-4c = -4c^2$$\n\n$$c = \\boxed{\\frac{b^2}{4}}$$"
