"To solve the equation \\((2^3)^4 = 2^n\\), we start by simplifying the left-hand side of the equation.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(2^3\\):\n\\[\n2^3 = 8\n\\]\n\nNext, raise 8 to the power of 4:\n\\[\n8^4 = 256\n\\]\n\nSo, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n256 = 2^n\n\\]\n\nWe know that 256 can be expressed as a power of 2:\n\\[\n256 = 2^{12}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the equation is:\n\\[\n2^{12} = 2^n\n\\]\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:\n\\[\n12 = n\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(n\\) is \\(\\boxed{12}\\)."
