"To rationalize the denominator of \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\), we need to eliminate the square root in the denominator. We do this by multiplying the numerator and the entire expression by the conjugate of the denominator. The conjugate of \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\).\n\nSo, we multiply the numerator and the denominator by \\(\\sqrt{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}\n\\]\n\nThus, the rationalized form of \\(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}}\\)."
"The sum of an arithmetic series is given by the formula $S_n = \\frac{n}{2}(2a + (n-1)d)$, where $n$ is the number of terms, $a$ is the first term, and $d$ is the common difference. In this case, we have $n = 15$, $a = 7$, and $S_n = -210$. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$-210 = \\frac{15}{2}(2(7) + (15-1)d)$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$-210 = 15(14 + 4d)$$\n\n$$-210 = 210 + 60d$$\n\n$$-60d = 420$$\n\n$$d = -\\frac{420}{60} = -7$$\n\nTherefore, the common difference is $\\boxed{-7}$."
