"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2x-4}}{\\sqrt[3]{x+4}} = 2\\), we can cube both sides to eliminate the cube roots:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{2x-4}}{\\sqrt[3]{x+4}}\\right)^3 = 2^3\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2x-4}{x+4} = 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by cross-multiplying:\n\n\\[\n2x - 4 = 8(x + 4)\n\\]\n\nExpanding and simplifying the equation:\n\n\\[\n2x - 4 = 8x + 32\n\\]\n\n\\[\n2x - 8x = 32 + 4\n\\]\n\n\\[\n-6x = 36\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = -6\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-6}\n\\]"
