"First, expand the left side of the equation:\n\\[\n(ax+b)(2x+3) = 20x^2 + 3ax + 2bx + 3b\n\\]\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n20x^2 + (3a+2b)x + 3b = 20x^2 + 44x + 21\n\\]\nBy comparing coefficients, we get:\n\\[\n3a + 2b = 44 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3b = 21\n\\]\nFrom the second equation, solve for $b$:\n\\[\nb = 7\n\\]\nSubstitute $b = 7$ into the first equation:\n\\[\n3a + 2(7) = 44 \\implies 3a + 14 = 44 \\implies 3a = 30 \\implies a = 10\n\\]\nThus, the sum $a + b$ is:\n\\[\na + b = 10 + 7 = 17\n\\]\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $\\boxed{17}$."
"We can use the quadratic formula to find the other root of the equation. The quadratic formula is given by:\n\n$$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\nIn this case, $a = 5$, $b = 4$, and $c = k$. We know that one root is 2, so we can substitute these values into the quadratic formula:\n\n$$2 = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{4^2 - 4(5)(k}}{2(5)}$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$2 = \\frac{-4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 20k}}{10}$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by 10, we get:\n\n$$20 = -4 \\pm \\sqrt{16 - 20k}$$\n\nAdding 4 to both sides, we get:\n\n$$24 = \\sqrt{16 - 20k}$$\n\nSquaring both sides, we get:\n\n$$576 = 16 - 20k$$\n\n​"
