"The given expression is a geometric series with the first term \\(a = \\frac{1}{2}\\) and common ratio \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). The sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series is given by the formula:\n\n$$S_n = a \\frac{1-r^n}{1-r}$$\n\nFor this series, \\(n = 10\\), \\(a = \\frac{1}{2}\\), and \\(r = \\frac{1}{2}\\). Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n$$S_{10} = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{1-\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{10}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{1-\\frac{1}{1024}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{1023}{1024} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{1023}{1024}$$\n\nTherefore, the value is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1023}{1024}}\\)."
"We start with the given value $f(14) = 7$. Since 14 is even, we apply the rule for even numbers:\n\n\\[ f(14) = \\frac{14}{2} = 7 \\]\n\nThis implies that $f(7) = 14$. Since 7 is odd, we apply the rule for odd numbers:\n\n\\[ f(7) = 3 \\cdot 7 + 1 = 22 \\]\n\nThis implies that $f(22) = 7$. Since 22 is even, we apply the rule for even numbers:\n\n\\[ f(22) = \\frac{22}{2} = 11 \\]\n\nThis implies that $f(11) = 22$. Since 11 is odd, we apply the rule for odd numbers:\n\n\\[ f(11) = 3 \\cdot 11 + 1 = 34 \\]\n\nThis implies that $f(34) = 11$. Therefore, the smallest possible number of integers in the domain of $f$ is $\\boxed{5}$."
