"We can solve the quadratic equation $x^2-5x-36=0$ using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\nwhere $a = 1$, $b = -5$, and $c = -36$. Substituting these values in, we get:\n\n$$x = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 144}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm \\sqrt{169}}{2} = \\frac{5 \\pm 13}{2}$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n$$x = \\frac{5 + 13}{2} = 9 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{5 - 13}{2} = -4$$\n\nThe largest possible value of $x$ is $\\boxed{9}$."
"To solve the equation \\((x+3)^2 = 121\\), we first by taking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\nx+3 = \\pm 121\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two possible solutions:\n\n1. \\(x+3 = 121\\)\n2. \\(x+3 = -121\\)\n\nSolving these equations separately:\n\n1. \\(x+3 = 121\\)\n   \\[\n   x = 121 - 3 = 118\n   \\]\n\n2. \\(x+3 = -121\\)\n   \\[\n   x = -121 - 3 = -124\n   \\]\n\nThe sum of the two values of \\(x\\) is:\n\n\\[\n118 + (-124) = -6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the two values of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{-6}\\)."
