"To determine the height of the pine tree, we can use the concept of similar triangles. Monica and her shadow form one triangle, and the pine tree and its shadow form another triangle. Since the sun's rays are parallel, these triangles are similar.\n\nLet's denote the height of the pine tree as \\( h \\) feet. The ratio of the height of Monica to the length of her shadow is the same as the ratio of the height of the pine tree to the length of its shadow. Therefore, we can write the proportion:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{5}{2} = \\frac{h}{34}\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( h \\), we can cross-multiply:\n\n\\[\n5 \\times 34 = 2 \\times h\n\\]\n\n\\[\n170 = 2h\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{170}{2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nh = 85\n\\]\n\nThus, the height of the pine tree is \\(\\boxed{85}\\) feet."
"To find out how many feet per second 66 miles per hour is, we can use the given information that 60 miles per hour is 88 feet per second.\n\nFirst, let's find the ratio of miles per hour to feet per second:\n\\[\n\\frac{R60}{R66} = \\frac{88}{60}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the ratio:\n\\[\n\\frac{88}{60} = \\frac{44}{15} = \\frac{22}{5}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\( R66 \\):\n\\[\nR66 = 66 \\times \\frac{22}{5} = 66 \\times 4.4 = 296.4\n\\]\n\nTherefore, 66 miles per hour is equivalent to \\(\\boxed{296.4}\\) feet per second."
