"The roots of the quadratic $3x^2+5x+k$ are given by $\\frac{-5\\pm i\\sqrt{11}}{6}$. We can use the fact that the sum of the roots of a quadratic equation $ax^2+bx+c=0$ is given by $-\\frac{b}{a}$ and the product of the roots is given by $\\frac{c}{a}$.\n\nThe sum of the roots is:\n$$\\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{11}}{6} + \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{11}}{6} = \\frac{-10}{6} = -\\frac{5}{3}$$\n\nThe product of the roots is:\n$$\\frac{-5 + i\\sqrt{11}}{6} \\cdot \\frac{-5 - i\\sqrt{11}}{6} = \\frac{25 + 11}{36} = \\frac{36}{36} = 1$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the roots is:\n$$\\frac{k}{3} = 1$$\n\nSo, $k = \\boxed{3}$."
"To find the domain of the function \\( h(x) = \\sqrt{25-x^2} + \\sqrt{-(x-2)} \\), we need to ensure that the expressions inside the square roots are non-negative.\n\n1. For \\( \\sqrt{25-x^2} \\):\n   \\[\n   25 - x^2 \\ge 0 \\implies x^2 \\le 25 \\implies -5 \\le x \\le 5\n   \\]\n\n2. For \\( \\sqrt{-(x-2)} \\):\n   \\[\n   -(x-2) \\ge 0 \\implies x \\le 2\n   \\]\n\nCombining these conditions, we get:\n\\[\n-5 \\le x \\le 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the domain of the function is \\( [-5, 2] \\).\n\nThe width of the interval is the difference between the upper and lower bounds:\n\\[\n2 - (-5) = 2 + 5 = 7\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the width of the interval is \\(\\boxed{7}\\)."
