"To solve the equation \\(\\sqrt{3x-5}=2\\), we first squaring both sides to eliminate the square root:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{3x-5})^2 = 2^2\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5 = 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(x\\) by adding 5 to both sides:\n\n\\[\n3x - 5 + 5 = 4 + 5\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n3x = 9\n\\]\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by 3:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{9}{3}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nx = 3\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the possible value of \\(x\\) is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
