"We start with the given equation \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 7 \\).\n\nFirst, we square both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n\\left( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right)^2 = 49\n\\]\n\nExpanding the left-hand side, we get:\n\\[\nx^4 + 2 \\cdot x^2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x^2} + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 49\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\\[\nx^4 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^4} = 49\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 2 from both sides, we get:\n\\[\nx^4 + \\frac{1}{x^4} = 47\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( x^4 + \\frac{1}{x^4} \\) is \\(\\boxed{47}\\)."
