"To find the person's daily caloric requirement, we need to determine the value of \\( x \\), which represents the daily caloric requirement.\n\nWe know that a snack-size tin of peaches has 40 calories and is 2% of the person's daily caloric requirement. Therefore, we can set up the following equation:\n\n\\[ 40 = 0.02x \\]\n\nTo solve for \\( x \\), we need to isolate \\( x \\) on one side of the equation. We do this by dividing the both sides of the equation by 0.02:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{40}{0.02} \\]\n\nPerforming the division:\n\n\\[ x = 2000 \\]\n\nThus, the person's daily caloric requirement is \\(\\boxed{2000}\\) calories."
"The population of bacteria doubles every four hours. Therefore, we need to determine how many times the population doubles in the twelve-hour period from noon to midnight.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of four-hour intervals in the twelve hours:\n\\[\n\\frac{12 \\text{ hours}}{3 \\text{ hours per interval}} = 4 \\text{ intervals}\n\\]\n\nSince the population doubles every four hours, the population will double four times in the twelve-hour period.\n\nStarting with a population of 600 bacteria, we calculate the population after each doubling:\n\\[\n600 \\times 2 = 1200\n\\]\n\\[\n1200 \\times 2 = 2400\n\\]\n\\[\n2400 \\times 2 = 4800\n\\]\n\\[\n4800 \\times 2 = 9600\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the population of bacteria at midnight will be \\(\\boxed{9600}\\)."
"We start with the given equations:\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 = 105\n\\]\n\\[\nx^2 + y^2 = 65\n\\]\n\nFirst, we expand the first equation:\n\\[\n(x + y)^2 = x^2 + 2xy + y^2 = 105\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute \\(x^2 + y^2\\) from the second equation into the expanded equation:\n\\[\n65 + 2xy = 105\n\\]\n\nNow, we solve for \\(2xy\\):\n\\[\n2xy = 105 - 65\n\\]\n\\[\n2xy = 40\n\\]\n\nFinally, we solve for \\(xy\\):\n\\[\nxy = \\frac{40}{2}\n\\]\n\\[\nxy = 20\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(xy\\) is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."
