"Given the equation \\(4x = 3y\\), we need to find the value of \\(\\frac{2x + y}{x - 2y}\\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\) using the given equation:\n\\[ x = \\frac{3y}{4} \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(x = \\frac{3y}{4}\\) into the expression \\(\\frac{2x + y}{x - 2y}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{2\\left(\\frac{3y}{4}\\right) + y}{\\frac{3y}{4} - 2y} \\]\n\nSimplify the numerator and the denominator:\n\\[ \\frac{\\frac{6y}{4} + y}{\\frac{3y}{4} - \\frac{8y}{4}} = \\frac{\\frac{10y}{4}}{\\frac{-5y}{4}} = \\frac{10}{-5} = -2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\frac{2x + y}{x - 2y}\\) is \\(\\boxed{-2}\\)."
