"To maximize the revenue, we need to find the price $p$ that maximizes the product of the price and the number of books sold. The revenue function is given by:\n\n$$R(p) = p(120 - 3p) = 120p - 3p^2.$$\n\nTo find the price that maximizes the revenue, we take the derivative of $R(p)$ with respect to $p$ and set it equal to zero:\n\n$$R'(p) = 120 - 6p = 0.$$\n\nSolving for $p$, we get:\n\n$$p = 20.$$\n\nTo confirm that this is a maximum, we can check the second derivative of $R(p)$:\n\n$$R''(p) = -6.$$\n\nSince $R''(p)$ is negative, $R(p)$ has a maximum at $p = 20$.\n\nTherefore, the price that the store should charge to maximize its revenue is $\\boxed{20}$ dollars."
"Let $c$ be the number of lemon candies, $p$ be the number of peppermint candies, and $v$ be the number vanilla candies, and $x$ be the number of chocolate candies. We have the following equations:\n\n1. $c + p + v + x = 15$\n2. $p + c = 2(x + v)$ \n3. $p = c + 8$\n\nSubstituting $p = c + 8$ into the first equation, we get:\n\n$$c + (c + 8) + v + x = 15 \\Rightarrow 2c + v + x = 7$$\n\nSubstituting $p = c + 8$ into the second equation, we get:\n\n$$(c + 8) + c = 2(x + v) \\Rightarrow 2c + 8 = 2(x + v)$$\n\nSubstituting $2c + v + x = 7$ into the second equation, we get:\n\n$$2c + 8 = 2(7)\\Rightarrow c = 1$$\n\nTherefore, the number of lemon candies is $\\boxed{1}$."
