"First, expand the product $(x-2)^2(x + 2)^2$:\n\n\\[\n(x-2)^2(x + 2)^2 = (x^2 - 4x + 4)(x^2 + 4x + 4)\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply the two polynomials:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 - 4x + 4)(x^2 + 4x + 4) = x^4 - 0x^2 + 16\n\\]\n\nThe resulting expression is $x^4 + 16$. The nonzero coefficients are $1$ and $16$.\n\nThe product of the nonzero coefficients is:\n\n\\[\n1 \\times 16 = 16\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the nonzero coefficients is $\\boxed{16}$."
"To find the value of the expression \\(-a - b^2 + 3ab\\) given \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = 5\\), we substitute these values into the expression and simplify step by step.\n\nFirst, substitute \\(a = -1\\) and \\(b = 5\\) into the expression:\n\\[\n-(-1) - 5^2 + 3(-1)(5)\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate each term:\n\\[\n-(-1) = 1\n\\]\n\\[\n5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\\[\n3(-1)(5) = -15\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute these values back into the expression:\n\\[\n1 - 25 - 15\n\\]\n\nCombine the terms:\n\\[\n1 - 25 - 15 = -39\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is \\(\\boxed{-39}\\)."
"We know that $f^{-1}(x)=f(x+1)$, so we can substitute $f(x)$ for $f^{-1}(x)$ and $f(x+1)$ for $f(x+1)$ to get\n\n$$f(x)=f(x+1)$$\n\nSubstituting $f(x)=5x-12$ into the equation, we get\n\n$$5x-12=5(x+1)-12$$\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get\n\n$$5x-12=5x-5-12$$\n\n$$5x-12=5x-17$$\n\n$$0=5x-17$$\n\n$$5x=17$$\n\n$$x=\\frac{17}{5}$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{\\frac{17}{5}}$."
"First, we solve the equation $|x-4| - 10 = 2$.\n\nAdding 10 to both sides:\n\\[ |x-4| = 12 \\]\n\nThis absolute value equation gives us two possible cases:\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\nSolving each case:\n1. \\( x-4 = 12 \\)\n\\[ => x = 16 \\]\n\n2. \\( x-4 = -12 \\)\n\\[ => x = -8 \\]\n\nNow, we find the product of all possible values of $x$:\n\\[ 16 \\times (-8) = -128 \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of all possible values of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{-128}\\)."
