"To solve the inequality \\((x-4)^2 \\le 9\\), we start by taking the square root of both sides:\n\n\\[\n|x-4| \\le 3\n\\]\n\nThis absolute value inequality can be split into two separate inequalities:\n\n\\[\nx-4 \\le 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x-4 \\ge -3\n\\]\n\nSolving these inequalities, we get:\n\n\\[\nx \\le 7 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x \\ge 1\n\\]\n\nThus, the solution to the inequality \\((x-4)^2 \\le 9\\) is \\(1 \\le x \\le 7\\).\n\nThe length of the segment on the number line that satisfies this inequality is the difference between the upper and lower bounds of the interval:\n\n\\[\n7 - 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the segment is \\(\\boxed{6}\\)."
