"To find the probability that Joan will solve the problem before her sixth try, we need to consider the probability that she does not solve the problem on the first five days and then solves it on the sixth day.\n\nThe probability of not solving the problem on any given day is \\(1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}\\).\n\nThe probability of not solving the problem on the first five days is:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)^5 = \\frac{243}{256}\n\\]\n\nThe probability of solving the problem on the sixth day is \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\).\n\nTherefore, the probability that Joan will solve the problem before her sixth try is:\n\\[\n\\frac{243}{256} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{243}{1024}\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{243}{1024}\\)."
