"To determine the probability that the correct sequence of answers is TFTFT, we need to consider the process of flipping a coin and writing each statement. Each flip of the coin has two possible outcomes: heads (T) or tails (F).\n\nThe sequence TFTFT means:\n- First statement: T\n- Second statement: F\n- Third statement: F\n- Fourth statement: F\n- Fifth statement: T\n\nSince each flip is independent, the probability of getting heads (T) on any given flip is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\), and the probability of getting tails (F) on any given flip is also \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\).\n\nThe probability of the specific sequence TFTFT is the product of the probabilities of each individual flip:\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^5 = \\frac{1}{32}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the correct sequence of answers is TFTFT is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{32}}\\)."
"We need to allocate 4 pens to dogs, 3 pens to cats, and 1 pen to roosters out of 8 pens. This is a problem of distributing 8 pens into 3 distinct groups (dogs, cats, and roosters).\n\nThe number of ways to distribute 8 pens into 3 groups is given by the multinomial coefficient of 8 choose 4, 3, and 1, which is calculated as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8!}{4! 3! 1!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating this:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{8!}{4! 3! 1!} = \\frac{40320}{24 \\times 6 \\times 1} = \\frac{40320}{144} = 300\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of ways to allocate the pens is \\(\\boxed{300}\\)."
"To determine the number of ways Kim can distribute 10 identical lamps on 3 identical tables, we can use the concept of \"stars and bars\" in combinatorics.\n\nThe stars and bars theorem states that the number of ways to distribute \\( n \\) identical items into \\( k \\) distinct groups is given by the binomial coefficient:\n\\[\n\\binom{n+k-1}{k-1}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\( n = 10 \\) (lamps) and \\( k = 3 \\) (tables). Therefore, we need to calculate:\n\\[\n\\binom{10+3-1}{3-1} = \\binom{12}{2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the binomial coefficient:\n\\[\n\\binom{12}{2} = \\frac{12!}{2!(12-2)!} = \\frac{12 \\times 11}{2 \\times 1} = 66\n\\]\n\nThus, the number of ways to distribute 10 identical lamps on 3 identical tables is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{66}\n\\]"
