"To determine the probability that a log in pond \\( A \\) will end up in pond \\( B \\), we need to analyze the possible paths the log can take from pond \\( A \\) to pond \\( B \\).\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that there are two possible paths from pond \\( A \\) to pond \\( B \\):\n1. Path \\( A \\to J \\to B \\)\n2. Path \\( A \\to K \\to B \\)\n\nSince the logs are equally likely to use any available exit flume, each path has an equal probability of being chosen.\n\nTherefore, the probability that a log will end up in pond \\( A \\) from pond \\( B \\) is the sum of the probabilities of the two possible paths:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that a log in pond \\( A \\) will end up in pond \\( B \\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\]"
"To find the probability that Larry Ponds will earn a walk exactly once in his next two plate appearances, we need to consider the probability of earning a walk on the first plate appearance and not earning a walk on the second plate appearance.\n\nThe probability of earning a walk on the first plate appearance is \\(\\frac{2}{5}\\).\n\nThe probability of not earning a walk on the second plate appearance is \\(1 - \\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{3}{5}\\).\n\nThe probability of earning a walk exactly once in the next two plate appearances is the product of the probabilities of each individual event:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{5} \\times \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{6}{25}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that Larry Ponds will earn a walk exactly once in his next two plate appearances is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{6}{25}}\\)."
