"To determine how many boxes contain neither pens nor pencils, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion.\n\nFirst, let's define the sets:\n- \\( P \\) is the set of boxes that contain pencils.\n- \\( Q \\) is the set of boxes that contain pens.\n\nWe are given:\n- \\( |P| = 5 \\)\n- \\( |Q| = 4 \\)\n- \\( |P \\cap Q| = 2 \\)\n\nThe principle of inclusion and exclusion for two sets states:\n\n\\[ |P \\cup Q| = |P| + |Q| - |P \\cap Q| \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\n\\[ |P \\cup Q| = 5 + 4 - 2 = 7 \\]\n\nSo, 7 boxes contain either pencils or pens or both. Since Roslyn has 10 boxes, the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is:\n\n\\[ 10 - 7 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of boxes that contain neither pens nor pencils is \\(\\boxed{3}\\)."
