"We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor of $13n + 8$ and $5n + 3$.\n\nFirst, we divide $13n + 8$ by $5n + 3$:\n\n$$13n + 8 = 2(5n + 3) + 2$$\n\nSo, the greatest common divisor of $13n + 8$ and $5n + 3$ is the same as the greatest common divisor of $5n + 3$ and $2$.\n\nNext, we divide $5n + 3$ by $2$:\n\n$$5n + 3 = 2(2n + 1) + 1$$\n\nSo, the greatest common divisor of $5n + 3$ and $2$ is the same as the greatest common divisor of $2$ and $1$.\n\nThe greatest common divisor of $2$ and $1$ is $1$.\n\nTherefore, the maximum possible value that the greatest common divisor of $13n + 8$ and $5n + 3$ can take is $\\boxed{1}$."
