"To find the length of the altitude $AD$ in the isosceles triangle $ABC$ with $AB = AC = 25$ and $BC = 14$, we can use the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nFirst, we note that $AD$ bisects the base $BC$, so $BD = DC = \\frac{BC}{2} = \\frac{14}{2} = 7$.\n\nNext, we apply the Pythagorean theorem to right triangle $ABD$:\n\n$$AB^2 = AD^2 + BD^2$$\n$$25^2 = AD^2 + 7^2$$\n$$625 = AD^2 + 49$$\n$$AD^2 = 625 - 49$$\n$$AD^2 = 576$$\n$$AD = \\sqrt{576}$$\n$$AD = 24$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the altitude $AD$ is $\\boxed{24}$."
