"To determine the number of different shows Elodie can put on, we need to consider the number of ways to assign the remaining three outfits to the five models. This is a permutation problem because the order of assigning the outfits matters.\n\nFirst, we choose 3 out of the 5 models to wear the outfits. The number of ways to choose 3 models out of 5 is given by the combination formula:\n\\[\n\\binom{5}{3} = \\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!} = \\frac{5 \\times 4 \\times 3}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 10\n\\]\n\nNext, we assign the 3 outfits to these 3 chosen models. The number of ways to assign 3 distinct outfits to 3 models is given by the permutation formula:\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of different shows Elodie can put on is:\n\\[\n10 \\times 6 = 60\n\\]\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\boxed{60}\\)."
