"The area of a trapezoid is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (\\text{Base}_1 + \\text{Base}_2) \\times \\text{Height}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the bases of the trapezoid are 9 cm and 3 cm, and the height is 5 cm. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (9 + 3) \\times 5\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 5\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = 6 \\times 5\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = 30\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the trapezoid is \\(\\boxed{30}\\) square centimeters."
"To determine the next time the two cyclists cross the starting line together, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of their lap times. The first cyclist takes 12 minutes per lap, and the second cyclist takes 16 minutes per lap.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of the lap times:\n\\[ 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\]\n\\[ 16 = 2^4 \\]\n\nThe LCM is found by taking the highest power of each prime that appears in the factorizations:\n\\[ \\text{LCM} = 2^4 \\times 3 = 16 \\times 3 = 48 \\]\n\nSo, the LCM of 12 and 16 is 48 minutes. This means the two cyclists will cross the starting line together every 48 minutes.\n\nStarting from 12:15 PM, we add 48 minutes:\n\\[ 12:15 + 48 = 1:03 \\]\n\nTherefore, the next time they cross the starting line together is:\n\\[ \\boxed{1:03} \\]"
