"To determine the weight of walnuts in the mixture, we first need to understand the total ratio of the mixture. The ratio of pecans, walnuts, and cashews is \\(2:3:1\\). This means the total number of parts in the mixture is \\(2 + 3 + 1 = 6\\).\n\nGiven that the total weight of the mixture is 9 pounds, we can find the weight of one part by dividing the total weight by the total number of parts:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Weight of one part} = \\frac{9 \\text{ pounds}}{6} = 1.5 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nSince the walnuts make up 3 parts of the mixture, we calculate the weight of the walnuts as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Weight of walnuts} = 3 \\times 1.5 \\text{ pounds} = 4.5 \\text{ pounds}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of the walnuts in the mixture is \\(\\boxed{4.5}\\) pounds."
