"First, we find the length of the hypotenuse of the right triangle using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\\[\n\\text{Hypotenuse} = \\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the square constructed on the hypotenuse:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square} = 5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\nThen, we calculate the area of the right triangle:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the triangle} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 4 = 6\n\\]\n\nFinally, we find the area of the pentagon by subtracting the area of the right triangle from the area of the square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the pentagon} = 25 - 6 = 19\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of the pentagon \\(ABCDE\\) is \\(\\boxed{19}\\) square units."
"To add the fractions \\(\\frac{}{4}\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\), we need to find a common denominator. The least common denominator (LCD) of 4 and 8 is 8.\n\nFirst, we convert each fraction to have the denominator of 8:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{3 \\times 2}{4 \\times 2} = \\frac{6}{8}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{1 \\times 1}{8 \\times 1} = \\frac{1}{8}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{8} + \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{6 + 1}{8} = \\frac{7}{8}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\) and \\(\\frac{1}{8}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{7}{8}}\\)."
"To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 180 and 450, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of 0.\n\n1. Divide 450 by 180 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   450 \\div 180 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 90\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, divide 180 by 90 and find the remainder:\n   \\[\n   180 \\div 90 = 2 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 0\n   \\]\n\n3. Since the remainder is now 0, the GCF is the last non-zero remainder, which is 0.\n\nTherefore, the greatest common factor of 180 and 450 is $\\boxed{90}$."
"Since lines $l$ and $m$ are parallel and points $A$, $B$, and $D$ are collinear, we know that $\\angle ABD$ and $\\angle ACD$ are equal. Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\\angle ABD = \\angle ACD = 143^\\circ.$$\n\nAlso, we know that $\\angle ABD$ and $\\angle BCD$ are equal. Therefore, we have:\n\n$$\\angle ABD = \\angle BCD = 5x - 8^\\circ.$$\n\nSince $\\angle ABD$ and $\\angle BCD$ are equal, we can set up the equation:\n\n$$5x - 8^\\circ = 143^\\circ.$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$5x = 151^\\circ,$$\n$$x = 30.2.$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is $\\boxed{30.2}$."
"To determine the number of valid codes for the combination lock, we need to consider the constraints and the properties of permutations.\n\n1. The lock has 5 digits from 0 to 9.\n2. No digit can be repeated in the code.\n\nWe can calculate the number of permutations of 5 digits out of 10 using the formula for permutations:\n\n\\[\nP(10,5) = \\frac{10!}{10-5)!} = \\frac{10!}{5!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the factorials:\n\n\\[\n10! = 3628800\n\\]\n\\[\n5! = 120\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\nP(10,5) = \\frac{3628800}{120} = 30240\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of valid codes is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{30240}\n\\]"
"To determine how many crab pots have three different colored balloons attached, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the intervals for the red, blue, and yellow balloons. The intervals are 4, 6, and 10.\n\nFirst, we find the LCM of these numbers:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(4, 6, 10) = 2 \\times 3 \\times 5 = 60\n\\]\n\nThis means that every 60th crab pot has three different colored balloons attached.\n\nNext, we need to determine how many such pots are there in the first 600 crab pots. We do this by dividing 600 by 60:\n\\[\n\\frac{600}{60} = 10\n\\]\n\nTherefore, there are 10 crab pots that have three different colored balloons attached.\n\nFinal Answer: \\boxed{10}"
