"Let the two prime numbers be \\( p \\) and \\( q \\) with \\( p > q \\). According to the problem, the difference between these two numbers is 17:\n\n\\[ p - q = 17 \\]\n\nWe need to find the sum of these two prime numbers, \\( p + q \\).\n\nFirst, consider the properties of prime numbers. Prime numbers are numbers greater than 1 that have no positive divisors other than 1 and themselves. The only pair of prime numbers that differ by 17 is (29, 12). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[ p = 29 \\]\n\\[ q = 12 \\]\n\nNow, we calculate the sum \\( p + q \\):\n\n\\[ p + q = 29 + 12 = 41 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the two prime numbers is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{41} \\]"
"Let \\( T \\) be the total number of games Kent played on Saturday. Since Kent won 25% of the games, the number of games he won is \\( 0.25T \\).\n\nIf Kent lost 12 games, the number of games he won is \\( T - 12 \\). Therefore, we can set up the equation:\n\\[ 0.25T = T - 12 \\]\n\nTo find \\( T \\), we solve the equation:\n\\[ 0.25T = T - 12 \\]\n\\[ 0.75T = 12 \\]\n\\[ T = \\frac{12}{0.75} \\]\n\\[ T = 16 \\]\n\nSo, Kent played a total of 16 games on Saturday. Since he won 25% of the games, the number of games he won is:\n\\[ 0.25 \\times 16 = 4 \\]\n\nTherefore, Kent won \\(\\boxed{4}\\) games."
