"The sequence repeats every 26 numbers (13 up and 13 down). To find the $5000^{\\text{th}}$ integer, we need to determine the position within the sequence.\n\nFirst, we calculate the number of complete cycles within the first 5000 numbers:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{5000}{26} \\right\\rfloor = 193\n\\]\nThis means there are 193 complete cycles of 26 numbers each, and the remaining numbers are:\n\\[\n5000 - 193 \\times 26 = 22\n\\]\n\nThe position of the $22^{\\text{th}}$ number in the sequence is:\n\\[\n22 - 13 = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, the $5000^{\\text{th}}$ integer in the list is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"
"Let \\( S \\) be the sum of the scores of the 6 tests Heidi has already taken. If she earns 99 points on the final exam, her total score for the semester will be \\( S + 99 \\). The average score for the semester will be \\( \\frac{S + 99}{7} \\). We know that this average is 90, so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{S + 99}{7} = 90\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 7, we get:\n\n\\[\nS + 99 = 630\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 99 from both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\nS = 531\n\\]\n\nSince \\( S \\) is the sum of the scores of the 6 tests, the average score per test is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{S}{6} = \\frac{531}{6} = 88.5\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the average score per test prior to the final exam is \\(\\boxed{88.5}\\).\n\nThe final answer is $88.5$. I hope it is correct."
