"Let the side length of the square be $s$. The area of the square is $s^2$.\n\nWhen the lengths of two opposite sides are decreased by $40\\%$, the new lengths become $s - 0.6s = 0.4s$.\n\nWhen the lengths of the other two sides are increased by $50\\%$, the new lengths become $s + 0.5s = 1.5s$.\n\nThe area of the rectangle formed is $0.4s \\times 1.5s = 0.6s^2$.\n\nThe decrease in the area of the square is $s^2 - 0.6s^2 = 0.4s^2$.\n\nTherefore, the square's area decreases by $\\frac{0.4s^2}{s^2} \\times 100\\% = 40\\%$.\n\nThe final answer is $\\boxed{40\\%}$."
