"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{x}{4} = 14\\), we first find a common denominator for the fractions on the left-hand side. The common denominator for 3 and 4 is 12. We rewrite the equation with this common denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{x}{4} = \\frac{4x}{12} + \\frac{3x}{12} = \\frac{7x}{12}\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7x}{12} = 14\n\\]\n\nNext, we eliminate \\(x\\) by multiplying both sides of the equation by 12:\n\n\\[\n7x = 168\n\\]\n\nThen, we solve for \\(x\\) by dividing both sides by 7:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{168}{7} = 24\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{24}\n\\]"
