"To find the smallest possible product of one one-digit prime and two distinct two-digit primes, we need to identify the smallest one-digit prime and the smallest two-digit primes.\n\nThe smallest one-digit prime is 2.\n\nThe smallest two-digit primes are 11, 13, and 17.\n\nNow, we calculate the product of these primes:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 11 \\times 13 \\times 17\n\\]\n\nFirst, multiply the first two primes:\n\n\\[\n2 \\times 11 = 22\n\\]\n\nNext, multiply the result by the next smallest prime:\n\n\\[\n22 \\times 13 = 286\n\\]\n\nFinally, multiply the result by the largest prime:\n\n\\[\n286 \\times 17 = 4862\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest possible product of one one-digit prime and two distinct two-digit primes is \\(\\boxed{4862}\\)."
