"Let the side length of each square be \\( s \\). Since three squares are placed side by side, the length of the rectangle is \\( 3s \\) and the width is \\( s \\).\n\nThe perimeter of the rectangle is given by the formula:\n\\[\n\\text{Perimeter} = 2 \\times (\\text{length} + \\text{width})\n\\]\nSubstituting the given dimensions:\n\\[\n104 = 2 \\times (3s + s)\n\\]\n\\[\n104 = 2 \\times 4s\n\\]\n\\[\n104 = 8s\n\\]\n\\[\ns = \\frac{104}{8}\n\\]\n\\[\ns = 13\n\\]\n\nThe area of each square is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = s^2 = 13^2 = 169\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the area of each square is \\(\\boxed{169}\\) square inches."
"To find the area of the square, we first need to determine the length of one side of the square. The perimeter of a square is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Perimeter} = 4 \\times \\text{side length} \\]\n\nGiven that the perimeter is 28 cm, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[ 28 = 4 \\times \\text{side length} \\]\n\nTo find the side length, we divide both sides of the equation by 4:\n\n\\[ \\text{side length} = \\frac{28}{4} = 7 \\text{ cm} \\]\n\nNow, we know the side length is 7 cm, we can find the area of the square using the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\text{side length}^2 \\]\n\nSubstituting the side length:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 7^2 = 49 \\text{ cm}^2 \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{49}\\) square centimeters."
"To find the value of \\(\\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{1}{3}\\), we need to add a common denominator. The least common multiple of 5 and 3 is 15.\n\nFirst, we convert each fraction to have a denominator of 15:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{2}{5} = \\frac{2 \\times 3}{5 \\times 3} = \\frac{6}{15}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1 \\times 5}{3 \\times 5} = \\frac{5}{15}\n\\]\n\nNow, we add the fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{6}{15} + \\frac{5}{15} = \\frac{6 + 5}{15} = \\frac{11}{15}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\frac{2}{5} + \\frac{1}{3}\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{11}{15}}\\)."
