"To determine the probability that three students with different names line up in alphabetical order, we need to consider the total number of possible arrangements and the number of favorable arrangements.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of possible arrangements of three students. Since there are three students, the total number of arrangements is given by the factorial of 3:\n\n\\[\n3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine the number of favorable arrangements where the students are in alphabetical order. Since the names are different, there is only one way to arrange them in alphabetical order.\n\nTherefore, the number of favorable arrangements is 1.\n\nThe probability that the students are in alphabetical order is the ratio of the number of favorable arrangements to the total number of possible arrangements:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Probability} = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable arrangements}}{\\text{Total number of possible arrangements}} = \\frac{1}{6}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that the three students are in alphabetical order from front-to-back is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\n\\]"
