"To solve the equation \\(\\frac{x}{7} + \\frac{x}{3} = 1 - x\\), we first find a common denominator for the fractions on the left side. The least common multiple of 7 and 3 is 21. We multiply every term by 21 to clear the fractions:\n\n\\[21 \\left( \\frac{x}{7} + \\frac{x}{3} \\right) = 21 (1 - x)\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[3x + 7x = 21 - 21x\\]\n\nNext, we combine like terms:\n\n\\[10x = 21 - 21x\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we add \\(21x\\) to both sides:\n\n\\[31x = 21\\]\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by 31:\n\n\\[x = \\frac{21}{31}\\]\n\nThus, the solution is:\n\n\\[\\boxed{\\frac{21}{31}}\\]"
