"To find the positive difference between the greatest and the least member of the set \\(\\left\\{\\frac{3}{7}, \\frac{4}{3}, \\frac{11}{8}, \\frac{6}{16}\\right\\}\\), we first need to identify the greatest and least elements in the set.\n\nThe greatest element in the set is \\(\\frac{11}{8}\\) and the least element is \\(\\frac{6}{16}\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the positive difference between these two elements:\n\\[\n\\frac{11}{8} - \\frac{6}{16} = \\frac{22}{16} - \\frac{6}{16} = \\frac{16}{16} = \\frac{1}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the positive difference between the greatest and the least member of the set is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{2}}\\)."
"To solve the expression \\(3^{-4} \\cdot 3^{-8} \\cdot 3^{14}\\), we can use the properties of exponents. Specifically, we use the property that states \\(a^m \\cdot a^n = a^{m+n}\\).\n\nFirst, we combine the exponents:\n\\[\n3^{-4} \\cdot 3^{-8} \\cdot 3^{14} = 3^{-4 + (-8) + 14}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the exponent:\n\\[\n-4 + (-8) + 14 = -4 - 8 + 14 = 2\n\\]\n\nSo, the expression simplifies to:\n\\[\n3^2\n\\]\n\nFinally, we calculate \\(3^2\\):\n\\[\n3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]"
"To determine how many inches taller Yao Ming was than Earl Boykins, we need to convert their heights to inches and then find the difference.\n\nFirst, let's convert Yao Ming's height to inches:\n\\[ 7 \\text{ feet} = 7 \\times 12 = 84 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\\[ 5 \\text{ inches} = 89 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nNext, let's convert Earl Boykins' height to inches:\n\\[ 5 \\text{ feet} = 5 \\times 12 = 60 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\\[ 5 \\text{ inches} = 65 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nNow, we find the difference in their heights:\n\\[ 89 \\text{ inches} - 65 \\text{ inches} = 24 \\text{ inches} \\]\n\nTherefore, Yao Ming was \\(\\boxed{24}\\) inches taller than Earl Boykins."
"First, find the reciprocal of $\\frac{5}{6}$:\n\\[\n\\text{Recipro of } \\frac{5}{6} = \\frac{6}{5}\n\\]\n\nNext, find the reciprocal of the reciprocal of $\\frac{5}{6}$:\n\\[\n\\text{Recipro of } \\frac{6}{5} = \\frac{5}{6}\n\\]\n\nNow, divide the reciprocal of $\\frac{5}{6}$ by the reciprocal of the reciprocal of $\\frac{5}{6}$:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\text{Recipro}}{\\text{Recipro}} = \\frac{\\frac{6}{5}}{\\frac{5}{6}} = \\frac{6}{5} \\times \\frac{6}{5} = \\frac{6}{5} = 1.2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1.2}\n\\]"
