"To determine the percentage of students who chose candy E, we need to identify the height of the bar representing candy E on the bar graph and then calculate the percentage based on the total number of students.\n\nFrom the bar graph, the height of the bar for candy E is 5 units. The total height of all bars combined is 25 units.\n\nThe percentage of students who chose candy E is calculated as follows:\n\\[\n\\text{Percentage} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Height of bar for candy E}}{\\text{Total height of all bars}} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[\n\\text{Percentage} = \\left( \\frac{5}{25} \\right) \\times 100 = 20\\%\n\\]\n\nThus, the percentage of students who chose candy E is \\(\\boxed{20}\\)."
"We know that $\\frac{\\sin (2A + B)}{\\sin B} = 5.$ Using the angle addition formula for sine, we have:\n\n$$\\sin (2A + B) = 2\\sin A \\cos B + \\sin A \\cos B.$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite the given equation as:\n\n$$\\frac{2\\sin A \\cos B + \\sin A \\cos B}{\\sin B} = 5.$$\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n$$2\\sin A \\cos B + \\sin A = 5.$$\n\nNow, we want to find $\\frac{\\tan (A + B)}{\\tan A}.$ Using the angle addition formula for tangent, we have:\n\n$$\\tan (A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}.$$\n\nSo, we can rewrite the given equation as:\n\n$$\\frac{\\tan (A + B)}{\\tan A} = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}.$$"
