"We know that $\\tan \\theta \\sec \\theta = 1,$ so $\\tan \\theta = \\frac{1}{\\sec \\theta} = \\frac{1}{\\cos \\theta}.$\n\nNow, let's simplify the given expression:\n\n\\[\\frac{1 + \\sin \\theta}{1 - \\sin \\theta} - \\frac{1 - \\sin \\theta}{1 + \\sin \\theta}.\\]\n\nWe can rewrite this expression as:\n\n\\[\\frac{(1 + \\sin \\theta)(1 + \\sin \\theta) - (1 - \\sin \\theta)(1 - \\sin \\theta)}{(1 - \\sin \\theta)(1 + \\sin \\theta)} = \\frac{2}{(1 - \\sin \\theta)(1 + \\sin \\theta)} = \\frac{2}{1 - \\sin^2 \\theta} = \\frac{2}{\\cos^2 \\theta} = \\frac{2}{\\sec^2 \\theta} = \\frac{2}{\\cos^2 \\theta} = \\frac{2}{\\sec^2 \\theta} = \\boxed{2}.\\]Final Answer: The final answer is $2$. I hope it is correct."
